{"id": "helpsteer2_verbosity/test/352", "source": "helpsteer2_verbosity", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"what should I expect in a seminar on \\\"From axioms to synthetic inference rules via focusing\\\"\\n<extra_id_1>Assistant\\nThe talk will be about a new framework for adding inference rules to sequent systems, and how that framework can be extended to handle certain classes of non-logical axioms. The main idea is to add inference rules in a way that respects the well-established proof-theoretic properties of sequent systems, while still allowing for a broad class of inference rules. The speaker will demonstrate this using the example of a hypothetical syllogism.\\n<extra_id_1>User\\nhypothetical syllogism \\n<extra_id_1>Assistant\\nThat’s right, the talk will focus on a hypothetical syllogism, and will demonstrate how the new framework can be used to add a synthetic inference rule for it. The main idea is to add a new rule that respects the usual proof-theoretic properties of sequent systems, while still allowing for a broad class of inference rules.\\n<extra_id_1>User\\ncan you tell more about synthetic inference rules \\n<extra_id_1>Assistant\\nSure, synthetic inference rules are just inference rules that are added to sequent systems in a way that respects the proof-theoretic properties of the original system. In the case of the hypothetical syllogism, the new rule will add a new inference step to the usual sequent calculus, while still preserving the usual properties of cut-elimination and consistency. This will allow for a broad class of inference rules that are compatible with the sequent calculus.\\n<extra_id_1>User\\ncan you suggest references on this topic\", \"response\": \"Certainly! Here are some references on the topic of \\\"From axioms to synthetic inference rules via focusing\\\":\\n \\n\\n  1. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Sequent Systems\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Automated Reasoning, 2010.\\n  2. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Non-Logical Axioms to Sequent Systems\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Logic and Computation, 2011.\\n  3. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Natural Deduction Systems\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Automated Reasoning, 2012.\\n  4. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Linear Logic\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Logic and Computation, 2013.\\n  5. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Hybrid Systems\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Automated Reasoning, 2014.\\n  6. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Affine Logic\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Logic and Computation, 2015.\\n  7. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Intuitionistic Logic\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Automated Reasoning, 2016.\\n  8. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Classical Logic\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Logic and Computation, 2017.\\n  9. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Constructive Set Theory\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Automated Reasoning, 2018.\\n  10. \\\"Focusing and the Addition of Inference Rules to Type Theory\\\" by Dan Ghica and Paul Taylor, in the Journal of Logic and Computation, 2019.\\n \\n\\n These papers provide a detailed introduction to the focusing method and its applications in adding inference rules and axioms to various logical systems.\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "2", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
